1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,792
The saved Week 14 plan names an EENT lab and says the course begins a
zero-feedback standard. The specific station document was not yet
available in the saved school view, so these are original practice
stations based only on the visible objectives.

2
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:31,176
A no-prompt run tests whether the learner notices, prioritizes and
explains. It is not a reason to permit preventable harm. The
instructor retains a safety-interruption role; the learner receives
coaching and correction after the run.

3
00:00:31,176 --> 00:00:46,152
Before the clock begins, agree on the safe simulation boundaries,
available equipment and stop word. During the run, the observer gives
no hints; the learner must verbalize the reasoning that would
otherwise remain invisible.

4
00:00:46,152 --> 00:01:04,032
When a safety issue requires interruption, stop the clock, correct the
immediate hazard and record the point. Resume only if the instructor
decides the simulation remains useful. This is an original coaching
method, not a claim about the school’s exact formal protocol.

5
00:01:04,032 --> 00:01:21,576
During the run, write short observable notes: “kept child upright,”
“asked for a forced mouth exam,” “started rinse before product label
arrived,” or “released nose pressure twice.” Avoid vague notes such as
“bad confidence” or “not ready.”

6
00:01:21,576 --> 00:01:40,992
The accessible BCPEG page 80 groups deficiencies by likely impact and
includes sequence, safety and critical reporting. The unseen pages
274–276 may add criteria; do not treat this worksheet as their
substitute or assign an official passing score.

7
00:01:40,992 --> 00:01:54,432
Use simulation props only. Do not practise medication administration,
invasive airway actions, eye-object extraction or other procedures
outside a supervised school station and current authorization.

8
00:01:54,432 --> 00:02:10,608
Plan the route and roles before the patient contact: who is leading,
who obtains equipment, who communicates with the receiving team and
who monitors the scene. Clear role assignment prevents a single
learner from losing the whole patient while focusing on one local
detail.

9
00:02:10,608 --> 00:02:28,128
Scenario: the child remains awake, drools, leans forward and becomes
agitated when an examiner approaches with a tongue depressor. The
learner should identify the airway threat and stop the unnecessary
examination rather than trying to prove a diagnosis.

10
00:02:28,128 --> 00:02:47,256
A strong independent response explains why the child stays upright,
what observations can be obtained without provocation, how early
transport/support is organized and which change would trigger
immediate escalation. Do not give medication or perform a procedure
simply to fill station time.

11
00:02:47,256 --> 00:03:06,768
During the run, the child stops resisting and becomes quiet. The
learner must not use silence as the only improvement measure. Look
again at responsiveness, speech, respiratory effort, colour and air
movement; a fatigued child may look less active while becoming more
dangerous.

12
00:03:06,768 --> 00:03:23,040
State the trend aloud, describe the likely airway risk and use the
current service escalation pathway. Do not force an oral examination
to resolve uncertainty; uncertainty is a reason to escalate, not a
reason to provoke the airway.

13
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:39,768
Scenario: a worker has bleach splashed in one eye, and saline has not
arrived. The learning objective is to prevent delay: start suitable
clean-water irrigation while another responder identifies the product
and contacts the appropriate support resource.

14
00:03:39,768 --> 00:03:57,312
Protect the responder and prevent runoff from contaminating the other
eye or surrounding people. Use a gentle controlled flow. Continue
according to the chemical-specific advice; do not neutralize the
chemical or stop solely because the school timer reached 15 minutes.

15
00:03:57,312 --> 00:04:15,648
In the school rehearsal, verbalize the specified “at least 15 minutes”
saline target. In actual readiness reasoning, distinguish the target
from an endpoint: start clean fluid promptly, contact CliniCall as
required and follow exposure-specific guidance.

16
00:04:15,648 --> 00:04:32,304
The observer should note both parts: did the learner know the school
criterion, and did they avoid a harmful delay when saline was not
immediately available? This resolves the apparent conflict without
erasing either the school exercise or current service guidance.

17
00:04:32,304 --> 00:04:49,008
Scenario: after a workshop accident, a small object is visible at the
eye and the patient reports pain with blurred vision. Do not use
forceps, press on the eye, remove the object or perform a lengthy
visual-acuity sequence before transport planning.

18
00:04:49,008 --> 00:05:03,936
Protect the eye using an appropriate rigid shield if available and
prevent movement of the object. Assess for the same mechanism’s head
or facial injury, monitor the patient and arrange prompt specialist
assessment under current guidance.

19
00:05:03,936 --> 00:05:19,656
Scenario: a patient with a nosebleed is leaning back and repeatedly
checking whether bleeding has stopped. Guide the patient to sit
upright and lean slightly forward; demonstrate the correct compression
point and maintain pressure without intermittent checking.

20
00:05:19,656 --> 00:05:36,816
Ask about trauma, blood-thinning medicines and bleeding disorders
while pressure is maintained. Do not focus solely on the nose: assess
airway, amount of blood loss, vital signs, symptoms and relevant
chronic conditions, then follow the current pathway.

21
00:05:36,816 --> 00:05:52,152
Use an original practice case, not a hidden school case. Start a five-
minute timer only after the learner has heard the dispatch and scene
information. The goal is to structure a usable record efficiently, not
to reward typing speed.

22
00:05:52,152 --> 00:06:08,952
After the timer, check whether a reader can reconstruct what happened,
what changed, why an action was taken, what response followed and what
remains unresolved. Record the actual completion time separately from
completeness; one does not prove the other.

23
00:06:08,952 --> 00:06:27,648
The saved plan asks for a Sunday written self-assessment using
Appendix G and three weak points for Week 16 remediation. The official
appendix was not available in the source set. This is an original
three-gap preparation tool, not Appendix G and not a substitute for
the school form.

24
00:06:27,648 --> 00:06:46,896
Phrase gaps as observable actions: “I did not state the child’s
position as a risk cue before planning transport,” or “I delayed
irrigation while waiting for saline.” Then choose one repeated drill
and a clear success observation. Avoid labels about character or
confidence.

25
00:06:46,896 --> 00:06:59,664
Start with the learner’s account, then compare it with the observer’s
factual notes. Ask what the learner expected to happen, what actually
happened, and which alternative explanation or change they considered.

26
00:06:59,664 --> 00:07:14,328
Choose only a few high-value practice targets. Re-run the scenario so
the learner can demonstrate the changed behavior. This reduces the gap
between recognizing a mistake in discussion and performing the safe
action under time pressure.

27
00:07:14,328 --> 00:07:32,352
Do not collapse all EENT calls into one generic exam. The right
priority depends on the threat: airway compromise, ongoing chemical
exposure, globe penetration or hemorrhage. State the reason for the
first action and the observation that will change your plan.

28
00:07:32,352 --> 00:07:46,776
The official lab sheet and Appendix G must be checked when the school
releases them. Until then, use these original stations to build
independent reasoning, accurate PCR habits and a concrete list of
practice needs.
